  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nNear term through Friday: Multiple impulses in NW flow this morning\nover the northern Plains. Mid-level warm air advection is supporting\nshowers into east central MN, ahead of schedule according to 00z \nglobal models/CAMs. Based on upstream trends, expanded low-end \npops over much of the area today, starting as early as 12z over \ncentral WI. Some risk of thunder (10-20%) during the peak heating \nof the day, though earlier arrival of showers and thicker clouds \nlikely holds down intensity. Much stronger signal for stronger \nstorms is well to the south as yet another MCS tracks along edge \nof upper ridge. Main concerns from any storms will be lightning \nand locally heavy rainfall. HREF LPMM does show a few bullseye of \n1-2\" of rain across central WI. These higher end amounts would be \nrelated to any stronger and longer liver storms.\n\nShowers/Storms this Weekend: A progressive upper trough and\nsurface low with warm front/cold frontal passage are forecast to\ntrack across the state Saturday afternoon through Saturday night\nbringing rounds of scattered to widespread showers/storms. The \ngreatest instability and convergence with low-level jet is shunted\nwell south of the forecast area Saturday limiting the severe \npotential. Despite the presence of PWATs over 1.8\" (95th \npercentile) farther north, models and ensembles suggest the\nheaviest rain risk will also stay to the south. WPC agrees and \nhas shifted the previous Slight Risk for excessive rain back south\nas well. Peak of rain chances will be Saturday through Saturday\nevening, with most activity east of the area by Sunday morning. \n\nNext Week Outlook: Dry Monday, then northwest flow brings another\nshortwave trough across leading to next chance for organized showers\nand some thunder Tuesday. Early look points to lower instability \nto support much severe risk, but most Machine Learning output does\nshow small 5-15% chances with the stronger flow aloft. Beyond\nthis, as the subtropical ridge builds northeast during the middle\nand end of next week a more active pattern may develop across \nparts of Wisconsin as a baroclinc zone sets up over the state. Its\nway to early to assess any specific thunderstorm threats, but this\nwill be a time period of monitor, especially for those with outdoor\nplans during the second half of next week.",
